    "faq": {
      "type": "collapsible-tabs",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Does it actually protect the outfit underneath?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes \u2014 that's the core design goal. The waterproof TPU backing on the front panel and both sleeves creates a physical barrier, not a surface treatment. It's been tested against full-strength tomato sauce, beet juice, and yogurt without bleed-through. The sleeves cover the arms where standard bibs fail completely. For most meals, you wipe the bib down and move on \u2014 no outfit change.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Long sleeve bib vs silicone bib \u2014 which one?",
            "answer": "<p>They solve different problems. Long sleeve is for puree feeding and baby-led weaning \u2014 full body coverage when food is going everywhere. Silicone is for self-feeding and snacks \u2014 the pocket catches what drops. Most families use both. The long sleeve bib is the right call from month 4 through the BLW phase; the silicone bib stays useful longer as kids get more coordinated.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "When should I start using a long sleeve bib?",
            "answer": "<p>Month 4 is the right time \u2014 when puree feeding starts reaching the forearms. That's when a standard short bib stops doing its job. The one-size design fits through approximately 24 months, which covers the full messy phase through baby-led weaning.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is the waterproofing reliable wash after wash?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes \u2014 the TPU backing is sealed into the fabric, not a surface spray or coating. Avoid high-heat drying, which degrades TPU faster than low heat. Cold wash and low tumble dry maintains the waterproof performance through regular use for the full 4\u201324 month range.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How many do I need?",
            "answer": "<p>Most families find 3\u20134 works well for three meals a day. The bib wipes clean quickly and machine washes easily, so you're not stuck waiting on laundry between meals. 2 is workable if you're washing every day; 4\u20135 gives comfortable margin during high-mess phases like BLW.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Does the elastic cuff leave marks on baby's arms?",
            "answer": "<p>No \u2014 that's a specific design decision. Most long sleeve bibs use narrow elastic that imprints a ring on a chubby forearm by month 7. The Loulou Lollipop cuffs use wide, flat elastic with low compression. It holds the sleeve in place without digging in, even on babies with chunky wrists.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
